{"conjecture": "Software systems accumulate implicit invariants—unstated dependencies between components that are never documented because each individual change never violated them. Over time, these invisible constraints form a dense web where new modifications increasingly risk violating an unknown invariant, making change difficulty proportional to the number of historical edits rather than the complexity of any single feature. The system becomes hard to change not because any single decision was wrong, but because locally-correct edits progressively thicken a constraint graph that was never visible to any individual reviewer."}
